('replace', 283, [
 "Quantum mechanics is the rule book for atoms, light and everything built from them. Its rules are few, strange, and better tested than any others in science. This chapter states them, then shows them at work in matter that is cold enough, or clean enough, for quantum behavior to become visible on a large scale.",
 "Energy comes in lumps. In 1900 Max Planck explained the spectrum of glowing bodies by assuming that light is emitted in packets of energy E = hν, where ν is the frequency and h is Planck’s constant, about 6.6 × 10^{−34} joule-seconds. In 1905 Einstein showed that light also arrives in such packets, photons, which is why the photoelectric effect has a sharp frequency threshold.",
 "Matter has waves too. In 1924 Louis de Broglie proposed that a particle of momentum p has a wavelength h/p, and electron beams were soon seen to diffract from crystals. In a double-slit experiment, electrons or photons sent one at a time each arrive at a single spot, yet the spots build up an interference pattern. Neither “particle” nor “wave” alone describes that behavior.",
 "The theory describes a system by a wave function: a set of complex numbers, called amplitudes, one for each possible outcome. The Schrödinger equation fixes how the wave function changes in time, smoothly and deterministically. When a measurement is made, the probability of an outcome is the squared magnitude of its amplitude (the Born rule). Amplitudes add before they are squared, and that is where interference comes from.",
 "A system can be in a superposition of states that would exclude each other in classical physics, and two or more systems can share a joint state that cannot be split into separate descriptions. That is entanglement, the subject of {ch28}. Some pairs of quantities cannot both be sharp at once. For position and momentum the limit is Heisenberg’s uncertainty relation, Δx Δp ≥ ħ/2, with ħ = h/2π. It is a property of waves, not a defect of instruments, and it sets the size of atoms ({ch22}).",
 "Particles also carry spin, an intrinsic angular momentum that comes in whole or half multiples of ħ. Fermions, with half-integer spin, obey the Pauli exclusion principle: no two can share a state, which gives atoms their shells and makes matter solid. Bosons, with integer spin, crowd into the same state. The sections below follow that crowding into Bose–Einstein condensates, superconductors and lasers, and then into newer quantum phases. What a measurement actually is, and how the classical world emerges, is left to {ch27}."]),
